<h2>Old Norse</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Codex Regius of the Poetic Edda (GKS 2365 4to)</dt>
  <dd>
    Guðvarður Már Gunnlaugsson, Haraldur Bernharðsson and Vésteinn Ólason (eds.), <i>GKS 2365 4to: Codex Regius of
    the Poetic Edda, an electronic edition</i>, Editiones Arnamagnæanæ Electronicæ 3 (2024). Normalized text,
    lemmatization and morphological annotation by Haraldur Bernharðsson; electronic work by Tarrin Wills.
    Licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>; text and word analyses are used
    unchanged apart from
    layout. <a href="https://eae.ku.dk/q?p=eae/vols/xml/1">Download</a>.
    Used for all Codex Regius poems and prose. The reader shows the normalized level, which reflects late
    13th-century Icelandic (for example <i>að</i>, <i>um</i> where many editions print <i>at</i>, <i>of</i>), and
    follows the manuscript, so its readings sometimes differ from textbook editions. Words the editors supplied
    from other manuscripts are shown in italics.
  </dd>
  <dt>Sophus Bugge, <i>Norrœn fornkvæði</i> (Christiania, 1867)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. Transcription by Tor Gjerde, <a href="https://etext.old.no/Bugge/">etext.old.no/Bugge</a>. Used
    for poems outside the Codex Regius (Baldrs draumar, Rígsþula, Hyndluljóð, Svipdagsmál, Gróttasöngr) and for
    Sigrdrífumál 30–37, lost in the Codex Regius lacuna. Bugge prints these in manuscript spelling.
  </dd>
  <dt>Snorra Edda, ed. Guðni Jónsson</dt>
  <dd>
    Normalized text from <a href="https://is.wikisource.org/wiki/Snorra_Edda">Icelandic Wikisource</a>, where it
    is published under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> with permission
    of <a href="https://heimskringla.no/wiki/Edda_Snorra_Sturlusonar">Heimskringla.no</a>. Guðni Jónsson (1901–1974)
    prepared the edition; the rights status of his editorial work beyond the Wikisource licence is not
    independently established.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Henry Adams Bellows, <i>The Poetic Edda</i> (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1923)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain (published 1923; Bellows died 1939).
    From <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73533">Project Gutenberg #73533</a>. Includes his introductory notes
    and stanza notes. Bellows sometimes reorders or regroups stanzas; the reader keeps the Old Norse order and
    places each English stanza beside the Old Norse it translates.
  </dd>
  <dt>Arthur Gilchrist Brodeur, <i>The Prose Edda</i> (New York: American-Scandinavian Foundation, 1916)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain in the United States (published 1916). Brodeur died in 1971, so the translation is still in
    copyright in countries with a life + 70 term.
    From <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Prose_Edda_(Brodeur_1916)">English Wikisource</a>. Used for the
    Prologue, Gylfaginning, Skáldskaparmál and Gróttasöngr. Brodeur did not translate Háttatal, and no
    public-domain English translation of it exists, so Háttatal is shown in Old Norse only. Skáldskaparmál is
    aligned chapter by chapter; Brodeur’s chapters (I–LXXIV) group Guðni Jónsson’s 89 chapters differently.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Word analysis and dictionaries</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Word analyses</dt>
  <dd>
    Codex Regius words carry the edition’s own lemma and morphology for that occurrence. Elsewhere (Bugge and
    Snorra Edda texts, and words the editors supplied), the panel lists possible analyses: spellings that the
    Codex Regius edition annotates the same way, and inflected forms from Wiktionary
    (via <a href="https://kaikki.org/dictionary/Old%20Norse/">kaikki.org</a>, CC BY-SA 4.0). These are
    marked <i>candidate</i>: they are not chosen for context. The most frequent reading in the edition comes first.
    Capitalized words in the prose that no analysis covers are matched against Bellows’s index of names and
    marked <i>name</i>; they have no grammatical analysis.
  </dd>
  <dt>Dictionary lookup</dt>
  <dd>
    The edition’s lemmas use normalized spellings that the dictionaries do not always share
    (<i>loft</i>, <i>heift</i>, <i>ǿði</i> against Zoëga’s <i>lopt</i>, <i>heipt</i>, <i>œði</i>). When the lemma is not a
    headword, the panel shows the matching headword with a “Listed as” note. Compounds that no dictionary lists
    are shown as their parts, each with its own entry.
  </dd>
  <dt>Geir T. Zoëga, <i>A Concise Dictionary of Old Icelandic</i> (Oxford, 1910)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. Digitization by Sampo
    Silvennoinen, <a href="https://github.com/stscoundrel/old-icelandic-zoega">stscoundrel/old-icelandic-zoega</a> (MIT).
  </dd>
  <dt>Richard Cleasby and Guðbrandur Vigfússon, <i>An Icelandic-English Dictionary</i> (Oxford, 1874)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. Digitization by Sampo
    Silvennoinen, <a href="https://github.com/stscoundrel/cleasby-vigfusson-dictionary">stscoundrel/cleasby-vigfusson-dictionary</a>
    (MIT), from the Germanic Lexicon Project.
  </dd>
  <dt>Henry Adams Bellows, “Pronouncing Index of Proper Names”, in <i>The Poetic Edda</i> (1923)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain. Identifies proper nouns, which Zoëga leaves out. Where Bellows lists several people of one
    name, the panel shows the ones he cites in the pages of the poem being read.
  </dd>
  <dt>Wiktionary</dt>
  <dd>
    Old Norse entries by Wiktionary contributors, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA
    4.0</a>, extracted by Wiktextract (kaikki.org). The declension and conjugation tables come from its inflection
    tables, in Wiktionary’s normalized spelling. Where Wiktionary has no table, nouns, adjectives and verbs get a
    table generated from Zoëga’s headword and grammar note, using only inflection patterns that reproduced
    Wiktionary’s tables for at least 95% of the words tested; the caption says so. Middle-voice forms of generated
    verbs are derived from the active forms under the same test. Compound adjectives and nouns with no table of
    their own are declined like their last part, under the same test. Proper names can instead get a “name” table
    generated from their ending and gender, checked against the edition’s attested forms. Otherwise the panel shows
    only the forms the Codex Regius edition annotates.
  </dd>
</dl>

